<!-- Website teaching layer for kotlin/runtime-and-jvm/jvm-interop.md. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">Your team ships a Kotlin analytics SDK. Kotlin callers write <code>TelemetryClient.event("signup")</code>. The first Java customer writes <code>TelemetryClient.INSTANCE.event("signup", Collections.emptyMap())</code> — and a week later reports a crash from a Kotlin <code>String</code> that their Java code passed as <code>null</code>.</p>

Kotlin and Java share one runtime, so calls cross between them freely — but each language has features the other lacks. Kotlin **lowers** its features (objects, companions, defaults, `internal`, properties) into ordinary JVM shapes, and offers `@Jvm*` annotations to make those shapes pleasant for Java. In the other direction, Java's missing nullability arrives in Kotlin as **platform types**. This chapter maps both directions so you can predict, and design, what the other side sees.

<!-- @after Overview :: visual -->
### Two directions across one seam

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-cols" style="--cols:2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Java → Kotlin</span><div class="node" data-state="warn"><strong>Unannotated Java returns <code>String</code></strong><small>Kotlin sees <code>String!</code>: null-safety is suspended</small></div><div class="node is-key"><strong>Your defence</strong><small>annotations on the Java side (JSpecify), or an explicit <code>String?</code> at the boundary</small></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Kotlin → Java</span><div class="node" data-state="warn"><strong>Kotlin features Java lacks</strong><small>objects, companions, default arguments, <code>internal</code>, top-level functions</small></div><div class="node is-key"><strong>Your tools</strong><small><code>@JvmStatic</code>, <code>@JvmOverloads</code>, <code>@JvmField</code>, <code>@JvmName</code>, <code>@Throws</code></small></div></div></div><figcaption>Most interop bugs live exactly at this seam: a missing annotation on one side or the other.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @diagram f79ef8185c1b2d3d -->
### What a Java caller writes — before and after the annotation

<figure class="stepper" data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">Four Kotlin constructs, seen from Java</span><div class="stage viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-el="obj"><strong><code>object SessionManager { fun current() }</code></strong><small data-text="objJava">Java: SessionManager.INSTANCE.current()</small></div><div class="node" data-el="comp"><strong><code>class Config { companion object { fun load() } }</code></strong><small data-text="compJava">Java: Config.Companion.load()</small></div><div class="node" data-el="top"><strong>top-level <code>fun titleCase()</code> in <code>TextUtils.kt</code></strong><small data-text="topJava">Java: TextUtilsKt.titleCase(s)</small></div><div class="node" data-el="def"><strong><code>fun connect(host, port = 80, useTls = false)</code></strong><small data-text="defJava">Java: connect(host, 80, false) — every argument, every time</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{"label":"Default lowering","caption":"Without annotations, each construct becomes a valid but awkward Java API: the singleton's INSTANCE field, the Companion object, the FileNameKt class, and no default arguments.","add":["obj","comp","top","def"],"state":{"obj":"warn","comp":"warn","top":"warn","def":"warn"}},
{"label":"@JvmStatic on the object","caption":"@JvmStatic also emits a real static method, so Java can call SessionManager.current() like any Java utility.","state":{"obj":"done"},"text":{"objJava":"Java: SessionManager.current()"}},
{"label":"@JvmStatic in the companion","caption":"The same annotation in a companion object puts a static load() on Config itself.","state":{"comp":"done"},"text":{"compJava":"Java: Config.load()"}},
{"label":"@file:JvmName","caption":"@file:JvmName(\"Strings\") names the generated class, so Java writes Strings.titleCase(s) — and renaming the Kotlin file no longer breaks Java callers.","state":{"top":"done"},"text":{"topJava":"Java: Strings.titleCase(s)"}},
{"label":"@JvmOverloads","caption":"@JvmOverloads emits connect(host) and connect(host, port) as well. Only trailing parameters can be left out — Java still cannot skip port while passing useTls.","state":{"def":"done"},"text":{"defJava":"Java: connect(host) · connect(host, 443) · connect(host, 443, true)"}}
]</script><figcaption>None of these annotations changes how Kotlin callers see the API; they only add a better shape for Java.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after First Principles :: visual -->
### Where a platform-type `null` actually fails

<figure class="stepper" data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">One Java <code>null</code>, four Kotlin call sites</span><div class="stage viz-stack"><div class="node" data-el="src"><strong>Java <code>dao.findById(42)</code> returns <code>null</code></strong><small>no nullability annotation, so Kotlin sees <code>String!</code></small></div><div class="node" data-el="declared"><strong><code>val name: String = dao.findById(42)</code></strong><small data-text="declaredNote">you claimed non-null</small></div><div class="node" data-el="inferred"><strong><code>val name = dao.findById(42)</code> … later <code>name.length</code></strong><small data-text="inferredNote">type left to inference</small></div><div class="node" data-el="arg"><strong><code>greet(dao.findById(42))</code> where <code>greet(name: String)</code></strong><small data-text="argNote">passed straight to a Kotlin parameter</small></div><div class="node" data-el="nullable"><strong><code>val name: String? = dao.findById(42)</code></strong><small data-text="nullableNote">you said it may be null</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{"label":"The source","caption":"The Java method says nothing about null, so the compiler lets Kotlin treat the result as either String or String?. Your code decides which.","add":["src"],"state":{"src":"warn"}},
{"label":"Declared String","caption":"Kotlin inserts a check on this assignment. It fails here, naming the call that returned null.","add":["declared"],"state":{"declared":"error"},"text":{"declaredNote":"NullPointerException: findById(...) must not be null — on this line"}},
{"label":"Left inferred","caption":"No check is inserted: the assignment succeeds and the null travels on. The failure appears at the first dereference, which may be screens away from the Java call.","add":["inferred"],"state":{"inferred":"warn"},"text":{"inferredNote":"assigns fine; fails later with the JVM's own NullPointerException on name.length"}},
{"label":"Passed to Kotlin","caption":"A Kotlin parameter of type String is a non-null claim too, so the check happens at the call, before greet runs.","add":["arg"],"state":{"arg":"error"},"text":{"argNote":"NullPointerException: findById(...) must not be null — at the call"}},
{"label":"Declared String?","caption":"Now the null is an ordinary value, and the compiler makes you handle it: name?.length ?: …. Decide at the boundary, before the platform type spreads.","add":["nullable"],"state":{"nullable":"done"},"text":{"nullableNote":"compiles only with null handling; no crash"}}
]</script><figcaption>Each outcome observed by compiling a Java class and Kotlin caller with Kotlin 2.4.21 and running on JDK 17. Exact NullPointerException wording for the inferred case depends on the runtime.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Production Example :: check -->
**Check yourself.**

<details><summary>A Kotlin <code>object</code> holds an SDK's public functions. Java users complain about <code>INSTANCE</code>. What do you add?</summary>

`@JvmStatic` on each public function (and `@JvmField` or `const` for constants). Alternatively, publish top-level functions with `@file:JvmName`.
</details>

<details><summary>You rename <code>Util.kt</code> to <code>StringUtils.kt</code>. What breaks, and for whom?</summary>

Java callers and anything that names the class (reflection, keep rules): `UtilKt` becomes `StringUtilsKt`. Pin the name with `@file:JvmName("Util")` before renaming.
</details>

<details><summary>Is <code>internal</code> a security boundary against a determined Java caller?</summary>

No. An <code>internal</code> member compiles to a public method with a mangled name (<code>name$module</code>); a top-level <code>internal</code> function keeps its plain name and is simply public in bytecode. The Kotlin compiler hides both; the JVM hides neither. Use real module boundaries and checks for anything sensitive.
</details>
